WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.569
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
we're here with James Spader.

00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:05.004
One of my favorite things
when you're here

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.040
is to talk about jobs you've had
before entering show business.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.375
I did not know that you worked
at a record store.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.244
-I did. [ Laughs ]
-Where was your record store

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.879
that you worked at?
-Up in the Upper East side.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:15.114
3rd Avenue.
-Gotcha.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:17.983
And was this maybe -- Over
the time you worked there,

00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:20.553
you realized maybe not
the most legitimate business?

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:23.556
-No, no, no. No, it was --
I mean, it was -- [ Laughs ]

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:26.126
It was legit.

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:28.428
It wasn't like it was
ill-gotten gains.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:29.662
-Okay.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:30.763
-But, I mean, you know,
where it went with --

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:32.765
I -- It was -- The eye --
It was a little,

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:34.600
teeny independent record store,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.736
and this husband and wife
owned it,

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:40.606
and they spent every dime
they made

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:43.876
on cocaine and Quaaludes.

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:45.612
And they just
went back and forth.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.881
You can imagine what
their household was like.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.350
They're either, like,
pacing or asleep.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.718
'Cause it was cocaine
and Quaaludes.

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:54.621
Every bit of it.
So there was no stock.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:58.124
It was like -- It was the only
retail job I ever worked in.

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:00.960
And it was just calcifying.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.863
I mean, you'd be --
It was a time --

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:07.266
It wouldn't even have taken much
to fill the shop, you know?

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:10.336
Because it was so tiny.
And yet they, you know --

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:15.008
Like, I think when
I was working there,

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.610
"Some Girls" --
-The Rolling Stones album.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.112
-Album came out.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.648
I think we had like,
I don't know, four copies

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.582
of it or something.
I mean, it was just so pathetic.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:28.087
So people would come in.
They'd ask for a record.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.455
You know,
I'd do my due diligence.

00:01:29.455 --> 00:01:33.192
I'd walk them over to the bin.

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:36.328
I'd go, "Oh, no, we don't.
We don't have it."

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:39.566
And we'd have like maybe
another record by the --

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:41.367
"Do you want this one
by the same artist?"

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.004
It would be their
least-interesting record.

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.840
And it was just so --
So you'd just be standing there.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.209
Customers wouldn't come
after a while.

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:51.844
And there was a guy --

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:57.083
There was a guy behind the cash
register up at the front.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.752
I wasn't allowed to be near
the cash registers.

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:01.421
-So they had two staff members?

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:02.822
-There were two staff members.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:05.925
-There was a guy up there,
and who barely talked to me,

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.327
and I don't know
what he was doing up there.

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:10.897
And he was raised up.
It was, like, at the pharmacy,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.032
to steal Jerry Seinfeld's
great joke

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.334
about why do the pharmacists
have to be higher than us?

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.103
But anyway,
he was, like, higher.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.571
I was down on the floor.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.473
He was higher behind
the cash registers.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.476
And busy all the time
doing God knows what.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.777
It was before computers

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.279
and laptops and phones
and everything.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.913
I don't know what the [bleep]
he was doing.

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:32.818
But he was there so busy
all the time and so busy

00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:37.523
that he couldn't talk with the
only other person in the store.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:40.893
And -- And I would stand there
just waiting, you know, for --

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:42.161
And he played music and stuff.

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:43.829
He got to choose the music,
as well.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:45.598
And so I would stand there

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.099
waiting for customers
and everything.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.935
Or they would send me out
to buy Quaaludes

00:02:49.935 --> 00:02:52.972
because they --

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.374
You know,
they had lots of scripts.

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:57.277
I don't know where they were
getting these scripts from.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:58.745
But they had tons of scripts,
you know?

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.813
-Oh, so interesting.
So they would send --

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:03.049
This is -- Quaaludes
were prescription drugs.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:04.449
-Yes.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:05.718
-So they had scripts.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.286
-They never sent you out
for the cocaine?

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:10.122
-This was a long time ago.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:11.557
-They don't even make
Quaaludes anymore.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.591
-You can't. Yeah.

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:14.494
-But anyway --
-Yeah, I have tried.

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.863
-[ Laughter ]
-I did, too.

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:19.898
I went on a vacation years ago.

00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:23.302
Went on a vacation down
to Cabo San Lucas in Mexico.

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:26.205
Mexico, a country I adore.

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:28.140
Cabo San Lucas, not so much.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.942
I mean, it's sort of a --

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:31.677
I mean, it's basically --

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:34.847
At that time, it was
sort of resorts and stuff.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.416
I am not a resort person.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:38.417
We have one photograph
from the trip.

00:03:38.417 --> 00:03:43.856
It's just this dark, black spot
on a bright white beach.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:47.326
The black spot
is me under an umbrella.

00:03:47.326 --> 00:03:50.930
And anyway,
I was so board and miserable

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.965
that I killed
two whole days out of --

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.834
I don't know --
a five-day trip.

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:58.237
Two whole days in town
trying to go to pharmacies

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:00.005
because I've been told

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:05.110
that they sold Quaaludes
in pharmacies in Mexico.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.512
It was a bunch of bull [bleep].

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.514
-Were they --
And now here's a question.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.916
In Mexico,
are the pharmacists higher?

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:15.388
-[ Laughs ] No.
They were -- They were on break.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:19.925
Every pharmacy I'd walk into,
"The pharmacist isn't here."

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:21.994
-They were like,
"We have some girls."

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:25.431
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.065
-Anyways, this couple
would send me out,

00:04:27.065 --> 00:04:28.868
because, you know,
they had all these scripts,

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:31.604
but they couldn't keep
showing up at places

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.839
with different names,
so they were constantly looking

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:35.908
for someone else
who could be the name, you know?

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:37.643
You could be Bob and whatever.

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.311
-And as soon -- Like,
the minute you sold an album

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:40.680
and, like, it was cash in hand,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.715
would they, like, then take
the cash and send you out?

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:44.350
-No, they'd show up
at different times

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.918
during the course
of the day, of course.

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.186
-Yeah.
-Keep things going, you know?

00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:51.057
And so they'd show up
during the course of the day.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.658
You know, maybe twice a day.

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:54.760
Come up.
They'd only deal with him.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.595
The guy up there.
-Right.

00:04:56.595 --> 00:04:57.730
-Because he had the cash.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.131
And they would just
empty the drawer.

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:00.532
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.468
-They give you a reference?
-And that did not last long.

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:06.405
It was so -- Oh, my God.
It was brutal.

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:08.941
Really brutal.
-Well, as always --

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.578
-Where the hell
were we headed with this?

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.914
-Where we're always headed.
To the end of the segment.

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.148
You guys.

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.651
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:20.753
The 9th season premiere
of "The Blacklist"

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.488
airs next Thursday,
October 21st,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.989
at 8:00 p.m. right here on NBC.

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:26.392
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

